<article>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="txt">Two claims, only one of them made</span></h2>
    <p class="lede">Part one made a point of the memory store not needing a dashboard to stay trustworthy. Still true, still standing by it: a text editor and <code class="inline">git log</code> get you all the way there.</p>
    <p>But "doesn't need one" and "gets nothing out of one" aren't the same claim, and I only ever made the first one. A directory of markdown files is legible. It's not <em>convenient</em>. Reading commit logs cold every morning just to reconstruct "what's active, what changed, what needs my attention" is a real tax, and it's one a person keeps paying for something a computer could just compute once and hand over. That's the whole job of everything in this piece — dashboard, chat, calendar, all reading and writing the same substrate from part one, none of them a second source of truth. A lens. Not a dependency.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="num">01</span><span class="txt">The brief: the same principle, aimed at a screen</span></h2>
    <p>Every session opens with a generated brief — active projects, what changed last time, anything urgent — built the same way part one's "derive, don't hand-maintain" principle described: computed from frontmatter fields and git commit history, not read off a status document someone kept up to date by hand. Nothing here is new; a dashboard is just a second place that principle gets to pay off, this time as something glanced at rather than something a script consumes.</p>
    <p>Getting a freshness signal onto a screen surfaced a mistake the terminal version never had to confront. An early "how stale is this" indicator implied real-world recency — how long since something actually happened — when what it actually measured was documentation recency: how long since someone last wrote a sentence about it. The two diverge in a specific, misleading direction: a project can look frantic because every change to it touches a tracked file, while a project that's getting real, hands-on attention looks idle because nobody filed a note about a morning spent on it instead of at a keyboard.</p>

    <blockquote class="pull-quote">
      <p>The dashboard doesn't know what you did. It only ever knows what you wrote down.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>The fix wasn't a better metric — there isn't one available from inside a text corpus — it was refusing to let the label claim more than the data supports. "Busy lately" became "written about a lot lately"; "gathering dust" became "no word in a long time." Small wording change, real correction: it moved the signal from a false claim about the world to a true claim about the record, which is the only claim a system built entirely on what people chose to type down is ever entitled to make. Any derived indicator is worth this same check — does the label match what's actually being measured, or what you'd like it to mean?</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="num">02</span><span class="txt">Chat as a second write surface, same boundary, finer grain</span></h2>
    <p>A phone-accessible chat interface turns the dashboard from a read-only view into a second write surface into the same repo — talk to the store, have it write back, from wherever a laptop isn't. It reuses the trust-tiering idea from part one, but applies it one level finer than "which surface": within a single conversation, on a single surface, the write path branches by <em>what kind of file is being changed</em>.</p>

    <div class="panel">
      <div class="panel-label">write policy — enforced outside the model's control</div>
      <pre>on turn complete:
  changed = diff(before, after)
  for file in changed:
    if file.ext == '.md' and file.path not in code_dirs:
      commit_directly(file)          # memory: reviewed after the fact
    else:
      route_to_pr_branch(file)       # code/config: reviewed before it lands
  # the agent never calls git — it edits files;
  # a supervising process decides what happens next</pre>
    </div>

    <p>The chat model itself holds no git tool and no direct write access — it edits files the way any editor session would, and a process outside the conversation performs the actual commit once the turn is over, deciding the write path by file type rather than by asking the model to self-police. This is part one's "sub-agents return data; only the orchestrator writes" boundary, instantiated literally rather than metaphorically: the chat model is the sub-agent here, the surrounding server process is the orchestrator, and the write policy isn't a preference the model could be talked out of, because applying it was never the model's job in the first place.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="num">03</span><span class="txt">The gate that rejects, not deletes</span></h2>
    <p>Part one told the story of a bug where a pre-merge check accidentally destroyed the work it was supposed to be reviewing — a cleanup step ran on every exit path out of a function, including the failure one, so a failing check meant "revert everything, commit nothing, say nothing." That piece drew the lesson about silent failure. The lesson worth carrying into this one is about what a gate is actually for.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="mark">the rule</div>
      <p>A review gate has exactly two acceptable outcomes: pass, or reject-but-keep. The moment its failure path can make work disappear, it has stopped being a quality gate and become a shredder wearing a quality gate's name.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The fix was an ordering change, and the ordering is the generalizable part. The failing version ran the check first and only assembled a real commit if it passed — so a rejection had nothing durable to fall back to. The working version assembles a recoverable commit on a local branch <em>first</em>, runs the check against it, and only decides afterward whether that branch gets proposed further or just reported as "failed, here's where it's parked." Same check, same failure rate — the only thing that moved is which side of the check the point of no return sits on.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="num">04</span><span class="txt">The calendar: same instinct, a different domain</span></h2>
    <p>The last view is a calendar — a second, bidirectional interface for controlling when scheduled agent work happens, alongside dragging, deleting, and rescheduling by hand. Two decisions here are worth separating, because one is a restraint and the other is a reuse.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-head">Reuse: an existing sync standard, not a bespoke format</h3>
    <p>Rather than invent an event format and a bespoke sync protocol, this is built on an existing, widely supported calendar-sync standard — the same "don't build what's already solved" instinct that picked git over a bespoke memory format in part one. Any standard calendar client can subscribe to it without custom software on the reading end; the work is entirely in exposing the repo's schedule data through a protocol that already has decades of interoperable clients.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-head">Restraint: mechanical sync only, no interpretation yet</h3>
    <p>The tempting next step — let a model read a freeform event description and turn it into a new task — was deliberately left out of the first version. What shipped is strictly mechanical: reschedule, delete, edit a recurrence rule, and have that sync cleanly in both directions. No model sits between an event and an action yet. That's a real capability being withheld, not a limitation being tolerated — the interpretive layer needs a genuine pattern of use to design against, and guessing at that pattern now would mean building a judgment call before there's evidence for what judgment it should make. Ship the plumbing, live with it, let the actual use reveal what the smarter version needs to handle.</p>

    <p>One structural lesson came out of getting even the mechanical version live: a client library's "discover the available calendars for you" step can be structurally unable to see a calendar that was deliberately placed outside any single account's own space — a shared collection meant for more than one reader doesn't live in the path that kind of discovery walks, no matter how correctly the permissions are set. The fix wasn't a workaround inside the discovery step; it was skipping discovery entirely and pointing the client at the known location directly. Worth remembering generally: an abstraction that promises to find things for you is only doing you a favor when the set of things is actually unknown. When it's a small, closed, already-known set, asking the abstraction to rediscover it is pure overhead — and, occasionally, structurally impossible.</p>

