  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="num">05</span><span class="txt">Access grows sideways, not from scratch</span></h2>
    <p>One question every one of these views eventually raises: what happens when a second person needs to read or write this system? The honest answer turned out to be "most of that work was already done, by accident of how trust was modeled in the first place."</p>
    <p>Permission was never attached to an account. It's attached to the content — a flag on a file marking it as something that should take a deliberate act to open rather than something a reader drifts into, checked before that content ever reaches a model or a screen. Nothing about that check asks who's asking. So when a second reader shows up, the gate that already existed still works, unmodified — what's missing isn't a permission system, it's a thin layer of identity to tell two people's sessions apart, layered on top of protections that don't need to know the difference.</p>
    <p>That's a cheap kind of extensibility to buy, and it wasn't bought on purpose — it's a side effect of modeling trust as a property of the content instead of a property of the account holding the door. Worth doing deliberately next time, rather than noticing it as a happy accident after the fact.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="txt">Where this leaves the pair</span></h2>
    <p>Part one argues the substrate has to work with nothing on top of it. Part two argues that "nothing on top of it" was never actually the goal — it was the constraint that kept everything built on top of it honest. Delete every view in this piece tomorrow and the store loses exactly nothing except convenience. Which, it turns out, is the whole reason the convenience was worth building in the first place.</p>
  </section>

